\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{SU(2) and SU(3) heat-kernel gauge models}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{For #{n=2} or #{n=3}, take #{G=SU(n)} and its real Lie
algebra
##{\mathfrak{su}(n)=\{X\in M_n(\mathbb C):X^{\dagger}=-X,
 \ \traceX=0\}.}
Fix the normalization
##{\langle X,Y\rangle=-\trace(XY).}
This is a real symmetric bilinear form on that real vector
space: cyclicity of the trace and #{X^{\dagger}=-X}, #{Y^{\dagger}=-Y} make
#{\trace(XY)} real and symmetric in #{X,Y}.
Moreover
#{-\trace(X^2)=\trace(X^{\dagger}X)>0}
for #{X\ne0}. Conjugation preserves the trace, so the form is
Ad invariant. Compactness and connectedness of #{SU(n)} allow
\ref{fdip-00ML} to apply with this metric.}

\p{Choose any #{\tau>0}, set #{k=k_\tau}, and choose any
real continuous gauge-invariant spatial potential on a finite
graph. For example, with a finite set of oriented closed
plaquette paths and their ordered holonomies #{U_p}, one may take
##{V(U)=\beta\sum_p\left(1-\frac1n
 \operatorname{Re}\traceU_p\right),\qquad\beta\ge0.}
Endpoint cancellation makes a closed holonomy transform by
conjugation, so this #{V} is gauge invariant. The transfer
operator #{T=M_bC_{k_\tau}M_b}, with #{b=\ue^{-V/2}}, therefore
has the positivity and trace class in the continuum comparison of
[the gauge transfer](fdip-0080),
[Gauss projection and periodic trace law](fdip-0083),
[spectral Hamiltonian](fdip-0085), and
[link-reflected spatial positivity](fdip-0086) already proved
from the [spatial transfer data](fdip-007Z). Those arguments use compact Haar measure,
Peter–Weyl and the stated coefficient summability, and require
no further restriction to #{SU(2)}.}

\p{The temporal weight in this example is the heat kernel.
The separate [SU(2) Wilson weight](fdip-0082) has its own
coefficient proof. For #{SU(3)} the fundamental character
may be complex; the spatial potential above explicitly takes
its real part, while the temporal heat kernel is real by
\ref{fdip-00ML}.}
